      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2010 SEPAR. Published by Elsevier Espana. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "Label": "UNLABELLED",
            "content": "Few studies have comprehensively assessed the evolution asthma disease in recent years."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To determine changes in morbidity, lung function and quality of life and to establish the impact in terms of cost in a cohort of patients with asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Prospective, descriptive and realistic study that included 220 asthma patients evaluated 10 years after their inclusion (1994-2004). For all the patients, data for symptoms, lung function, quality of life and financial cost were collected."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "There was a decrease in the frequency of health service visits, including: emergency room visits for asthma exacerbations, 0.3 (0.9) versus 0.6 (1) visits per patient per year (P=.003); a reduction in the severity of the disease, with a greater proportion of patients with mild asthma, 121 (54.8%) versus 94 (42.7%) (P=.001); a decrease (improvement in quality of life) in the total SGRQ, 30.1 (16.5) versus 37 (19.6) (P<.001); and reduced total costs, 1,464\u20ac (3,415.8) compared to 2,267\u20ac (4.174) per patient/year (P<.001), mainly due to indirect costs, 617.50\u20ac (2855.9) compared to 1,320.10\u20ac (3,685.3) per patient/year (P=.001). When assessing the changes observed according to asthma severity, no differences were observed between groups."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The evolution of the morbidity and quality of life of asthma patients between 1994 and 2004 are clearly favorable. This improvement provided a significant reduction in the total costs of disease treatment."
          }
        ]
      },
